mmdevapi: run the audio loops on system threads (backport).

Backport of two upstream Wine commits that Proton 11.0 predates:

    a70f1eb8ee2  mmdevapi: Use a system thread for the audio driver main loop thread.
    4c3dd4e1d0e  mmdevapi: Use a system thread for the audio driver timer loop thread.

They are cherry-picked here because patch 0008 -- the AAudio driver -- is
written against the interface they introduce, and that interface is not
source-compatible with the one Proton 11.0 carries. Concretely, between Wine
11.0 and 11.14 the unixlib contract changed in four ways that all bear on a
driver:

  - `main_loop` became `main_loop_start` + `main_loop_stop`, so every entry
    from index 3 onward shifts by one. A driver built against the newer enum
    and loaded by the older mmdevapi does not fail to link; it silently calls
    the wrong function for every operation.
  - `timer_loop` was removed. The PE side no longer spawns the per-stream timer
    thread and call it; the driver creates its own and owns its lifetime.
  - `struct main_loop_params` and `struct timer_loop_params` went away with
    them, as did `release_stream_params.timer_thread`.
  - `create_unix_thread()` was added to unixlib.h as the helper a driver uses
    to make that thread. 0008 calls it, so without this backport the driver
    does not compile at all.

Adapting the driver backwards was the alternative and was rejected: it is
verified working on device as written, and the older shape is the one upstream
moved away from. Backporting the interface keeps 0008 byte-identical to the
version that was tested.

Only dlls/mmdevapi/ is taken. The upstream commits also touch winealsa,
winecoreaudio and winepulse, which build/wine.sh configures out
(--without-alsa, --without-coreaudio, --without-pulse) and which therefore
never compile here -- and wineoss.drv/oss.c, which 0008 replaces in full.

One hunk needed hand-resolution rather than a straight apply. Upstream's
DLL_PROCESS_DETACH reads

    if (lpvReserved) break;
    main_loop_stop();
    if (drvs.module_unixlib) { __wine_unload_unix_lib( drvs.module ); }

where Proton's calls main_loop_stop() *after* the block that does
FreeLibrary( drvs.module ). That ordering is harmless while main_loop_stop is a
PE-side function and wrong the moment it becomes a unix call, so the call is
moved inside the `drvs.module_unixlib` block, ahead of the FreeLibrary, rather
than substituted in place.
